WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.420
It turns out that the Netflix&nbsp;
comedy special "Chris Rock:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:07.500
Selective Outrage" was selectively edited&nbsp;
by the streamer, but it has nothing to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.520
with censorship. So why did they choose&nbsp;
to chop up a much-hyped "live" event?

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:16.200
When Will Smith slapped Chris Rock at&nbsp;
the 2022 Oscars ceremony, the incident&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:21.060
immediately became infamous, manifesting&nbsp;
memes, social commentary, and hot takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.280
from every corner of the internet. Following&nbsp;
"The Slap," Smith publicly apologized to Chris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:30.389
explained his actions, and accepted&nbsp;
his 10-year ban from the Academy.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:39.060
"My behavior was unacceptable, and I'm&nbsp;
here whenever you're ready to talk."

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:42.780
While Smith and his wife, Jada Pinkett&nbsp;
Smith, have been vocal about the incident,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:47.640
Rock was largely quiet... until now. As&nbsp;
a comedian, it's only natural that Rock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:52.440
would break his silence on that chaotic night&nbsp;
doing the one thing he knows best: comedy.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:56.700
The couple were a key part of Rock's new&nbsp;
Netflix stand-up comedy special, "Chris Rock:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.240
Selective Outrage." After detailing&nbsp;
his issues with expensive yoga pants,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.034
ruminating on American politics,&nbsp;
and debating controversial opinions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.034 --> 00:01:07.080
Rock spent the last minutes of&nbsp;
his show calling out the Smiths.

00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.340
Seeing as the world has been waiting for&nbsp;
the comedian's unfiltered thoughts on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:15.600
"The Slap," Netflix launched the special&nbsp;
with much fanfare, making it their very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:19.920
first live event to air across the world.&nbsp;
While it was a largely successful effort,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.468
the comedian's performance showed the difficulties&nbsp;
of performing live in front of millions.

00:01:24.468 --> 00:01:27.240
Near the end of the special, Rock&nbsp;
delivered a series of jokes about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:32.880
both Smith and Pinkett Smith, which included&nbsp;
a flub where he confused Smith's 2015 sports&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.120
drama "Concussion" for his 2022 slavery-era drama&nbsp;
"Emancipation." In the live stream, Rock said,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:41.760
"Years ago, his wife said&nbsp;
I should quit the Oscars;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:44.820
I shouldn’t host ’cause her man&nbsp;
didn’t get nominated for Emancipation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.156
the biggest piece of sh-t ever! No, not&nbsp;
Emancipation, I f--ked up the joke."

00:01:49.156 --> 00:01:52.156
After the mix-up, Rock did that&nbsp;
part of the joke over again:

00:01:52.156 --> 00:01:55.500
"She said, me, a f--king grown-ass man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:59.280
should quit his job 'cause her husband&nbsp;
didn’t get nominated for 'Concussion.'"

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.880
Rock was referencing Jada Pinkett&nbsp;
Smith boycotting the Oscars ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.448
during the peak of the #OscarsSoWhite movement.

00:02:05.448 --> 00:02:06.960
Despite Rock's quick correction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.800
Netflix decided to edit the flub out of&nbsp;
the on-demand version of the live program,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.430
according to The Hollywood Reporter, although&nbsp;
the exact reasons for the cut are unknown.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:19.980
Was it just an easy fix to make, so the special&nbsp;
would have more polish? Or was the streaming giant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.360
trying to avoid the backlash they'd likely get&nbsp;
for Rock roasting the historical slavery drama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:28.800
even if it was by mistake? While Rock&nbsp;
messed up the joke in a really awkward way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.735
it reportedly still received applause&nbsp;
during the original version of the special.

00:02:32.735 --> 00:02:36.540
The Hollywood Reporter confirms that Rock is&nbsp;
aware of the change and has worked with Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:41.400
to make the necessary edits. Viewers who decide&nbsp;
to watch the comedy special on Netflix's official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:47.017
website will see a disclaimer just below the set's&nbsp;
title, which reads: "Streamed live and edited."

00:02:47.017 --> 00:02:50.520
The special also no longer features&nbsp;
the "N Live" logo on the right-hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:53.220
bottom corner. In the final,&nbsp;
edited version of the special,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:57.267
Rock effortlessly glides through the&nbsp;
joke, never mentioning "Emancipation."

00:02:57.267 --> 00:03:01.860
Some fans may be disappointed that Netflix is&nbsp;
undermining the live and unfiltered aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:05.820
the special, especially after making such&nbsp;
an event out of it. It's unclear if Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:10.805
would have still edited the special in any&nbsp;
capacity if Rock hadn't flubbed his own joke.

00:03:10.805 --> 00:03:13.200
The publication also highlighted how "Chris Rock:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.920
Selective Outrage" had failed to land in&nbsp;
the streaming service's worldwide rankings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:20.787
even though it came in at 7th&nbsp;
place in the U.S. Top 10 rankings.

